<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        PRG's OWN tables in dcre_pay. PRG is their single writer (R-04).

        V1 BASELINE. dcre_pay is a new database being cut over directly, so this file
        declares the FINAL shape of each object in one typed changeset instead of
        replaying how CRG got there. Concretely, CRG reaches this same state through
        53 changesets across five files: prg_status_class is created with two columns
        and then gains classification, reportable and sla_suppressed by three
        addColumn + backfill + addNotNullConstraint triples; ACWC/ACWP are inserted as
        UNSUPPORTED by 004 and corrected to accepted-warehoused by 005; prg_report
        gains job_name by a later ALTER. None of that history exists here and none of
        it is reproduced.

        The usual re-run guards are therefore ABSENT BY DESIGN, and that absence is
        the point rather than an oversight:
          - no <validCheckSum>ANY</validCheckSum>: nothing has executed these
            changesets anywhere, so no checksum can have drifted;
          - no MARK_RAN preconditions guarding objects that "might already exist":
            on an empty database they cannot, and a MARK_RAN that fires once records
            the skip PERMANENTLY (A-79, A-81);
          - no defensive IF-NOT-EXISTS retrofits: DATABASECHANGELOG is what makes a
            re-run safe from a clean baseline, and it is sufficient on its own.
        There is no longer a contrast to draw with a bootstrap guard: on 2026-08-08 the
        nine relations PRG READS but does not own stopped being created here at all
        (see the ordering contract in db.changelog-master.xml). The read-path indexes at
        the end of this file are the one thing PRG still declares on another service's
        table, and they are a DECLARATION rather than a mint: an index cannot bring a
        missing table into being, so it fails loudly exactly like the views do.

        Table names keep the prg_ prefix, which here names the OWNING SERVICE rather
        than being inherited baggage: in dcre_col those objects belong to CRG under
        its former name, in dcre_pay they belong to PRG under its actual one. Two
        databases, so the two sets can never collide.
    -->

    <!-- ===================== R-21 status catalogue (reference table) =========== -->
    <!-- R-21: status classes live in a reference table, never in an enum. All
         fourteen recognised Fintegrate codes are explicit and fully classified at
         v1; an unrecognised code is NOT listed, LEFT JOINs to NULL and fails closed
         as a non-reportable protocol exception (prg_status_exception).

         ACWP/ACWC are carried even though payments cannot warehouse. They are RMB
         DebiCheck codes for future-dated and auto-bumped COLLECTIONS, and PRW's own
         changelog records that ENDO has no collection day at all, so they are not
         expected on this leg. They are listed anyway because the alternative is
         worse: an unlisted code that does arrive would be silently suppressed from
         every PSR as an unknown, and a catalogue that classifies a code it never
         sees costs nothing. -->
    <changeSet id="prg-002-status-class" author="dcre">
        <!-- A-82: this changeset gained a re-run guard AFTER it had already been applied,
             which changes the enclosing checksum without changing one byte of the DDL
             below. ANY rather than a pinned value: kind-dcre-dev stored 9:64276aa96effabe1738aacdff529d7a4,
             and every environment that migrated at a different time holds a different
             one, so a pin is correct on exactly one database and silently wrong on the
             rest. The preConditions block is what keeps the re-run safe; the checksum
             is not load-bearing here. Same call, same reason, as prr 002-batch-metadata. -->
        <validCheckSum>ANY</validCheckSum>
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><tableExists tableName="prg_status_class"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        
        <createTable tableName="prg_status_class">
            <column name="code" type="VARCHAR(8)">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="terminal" type="BOOLEAN"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="classification" type="VARCHAR(32)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="reportable" type="BOOLEAN"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <!-- A tx whose latest status is accepted-warehoused HAS its response and
                 must not age on the 20h/24h pending-SLA path (SCRUM-68). -->
            <column name="sla_suppressed" type="BOOLEAN"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
        </createTable>
        <rollback><dropTable tableName="prg_status_class"/></rollback>
    </changeSet>

